  "details": "Right now, the only way you can share an avatar with a friend is to either:\n\n- Give them the Unity project files, which requires them to install VCC and Unity, as well as possibly other dependencies\n\n- Log into their VRChat account, which involves the friend giving their login credentials to you, which is an extremely risky/bad security practice\n\n- Set the avatar as 'public', allowing it to be cloned by others, which allows for other parties to clone the avatar off of the friend and share the avatar beyond your control, and the possibility that the avtr_ ID will end up in public avatar search worlds. And yes, I know you can set a code, but that also can be distributed beyond your control.\n\nI've had to do option 3 for some avatars I share with my small group of friends who don't have the ability to create their own avatars, and unfortunately it caused me to receive a week long public world ban after one of my avatars spread beyond my friends (i take responsibility for this). \n\nBut if I had the ability to *only* allow specific users to use the avatar, this never would've been an issue. It would also come in handy for content creators who may wish to offer an un-customizable avatar for a lower price, without worrying about people cloning.\n\nIt would also help ensure \"Private content stays private\" per the recently revised Community Guidelines\n\nI realize this could also lead to people potentially violating model creators individual TOS's regarding licensing, but that is the responsibility of the user, not VRChat.",
